0,Nilo-Saharan languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nilo-Saharan_languages,"2 weeks ago - Dholuo language of the Luo people ... Southern Nilotic-speaking Kalenjin (the term ""Luo"" is also used for a wider group of languages which includes Dholuo.). Kanuri (4.0 million, all dialects; 4.7 million if Kanembu is included). The major ethnicity around Lake Chad. Zarma (6 million). Spread along the Niger River in Niger and into Nigeria, in the southern region of the historic Songhai Empire. Teso (1.9 million). Related to Karamojong, Turkana, Toposa and ...",Toposa,Nilo-Saharan
1,Toposa people - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toposa_people,"March 3, 2026 - The Toposa are a Nilotic ethnic group in South Sudan, living in the Greater Kapoeta region of Eastern Equatoria state. They have traditionally lived by herding cattle, sheep and goats, and in the past were involved in the ivory trade.",Toposa,Nilo-Saharan
2,Sub-Saharan African music traditions - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sub-Saharan_African_music_traditions,"April 15, 2026 - The musicology of Sudan involves ... Nilotic and Niger–Congo. In contrast to traditional Arabic music, most Sudanese music styles are pentatonic, and the simultaneous beats of percussion or singing in polyrhythms are further prominent characteristics of Sudanese music. Sudan takes its name from that of the sub-Saharan savanna which ...",Toposa,Nilo-Saharan
3,Toposa,https://www.101lasttribes.com/tribes/toposa2.html,"The Toposa culture is orally transmitted through songs, dance, music, poems and folklore. Being pastoralists, they have perfected their art of war and cattle raiding. They are able to spy and gather information about the enemy, water, pastures, etc. with precision.",Toposa,Nilo-Saharan
4,Turkana introduction - Traditional Music & Cultures of Kenya,https://www.bluegecko.org/kenya/tribes/turkana/,"Neighbouring tribes: Borana, El Molo, Marakwet, Pokot, Rendille, Samburu. Toposa (Sudan). Karamajong and Jie (Uganda). Language: Turkana (Nilo-Saharan, Eastern Sudanic, Nilotic, Eastern, Teso-Turkana). Similar to Karamajong and Teso. Related to Toposa. Two dialects (southern and northern Turkana).",Toposa,Nilo-Saharan
5,Nilo-Saharan Languages,https://languagesgulper.com/eng/Nilo.html,"•Nilotic (North Tanzania, West Kenya, North Uganda, South Sudan, Sudan and Ethiopia). It is the largest Eastern Sudanic group. It has about 18.7 million speakers and is divided in Western, Eastern and Southern subgroups: 1.Western: Dinka, Luo, Nuer. Totalling 9.4 million speakers. 2.Eastern: Bari, Kakwa, Teso (Ateso), Karamojong, Toposa...",Toposa,Nilo-Saharan
6,Hire Local Nilo-Saharan Music Acts To Hire | Daytona Beach FL,"https://acemusicbookingagency.com/nilo-saharan-music/?l=Daytona+Beach+FL,+Florida&c=United+States","December 31, 2022 - With available local talented musicians performing music in languages of Central and North-Central Africa (Kenya, Nigeria, Niger, Sudan, Uganda, Chad regions) in Daytona Beach FL, you can find the perfect performer for your event entertainment. Don't settle for just any event entertainment - book a great Nilo-Saharan musician for hire to elevate your event and leave a lasting impression.",Toposa,Nilo-Saharan
7,AFRICA | 101 Last Tribes - Toposa people,https://www.101lasttribes.com/tribes/toposa.html,"They believe that men originally lived with ""Nakwuge"" in the sky, but many slid down a rope to earth. The rope then broke, separating them from heaven. As of 2000, perhaps 5% of the population could read. The Toposa culture is orally transmitted through songs, dance, music, poems and folklore.",Toposa,Nilo-Saharan
8,Nilo-Saharan languages - Wikiwand,https://www.wikiwand.com/en/Nilo-Saharan_languages,"Dholuo language of the Luo people of Kenya and Tanzania, Kenya's fourth largest ethnicity after the Bantu-speaking Agĩkũyũ, Luhya and the Southern Nilotic-speaking Kalenjin (the term ""Luo"" is also used for a wider group of languages which includes Dholuo.). Kanuri (4.0 million, all dialects; 4.7 million if Kanembu is included). The major ethnicity around Lake Chad. Zarma (6 million). Spread along the Niger River in Niger and into Nigeria, in the southern region of the historic Songhai Empire. Teso (1.9 million). Related to Karamojong, Turkana, Toposa and Nyangatom",Toposa,Nilo-Saharan
